1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:07,350
This video shows you how to find the pipeline that made the records you read in your SIEM.

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:12,470
Your SIEM reads firewall records from Elasticsearch, and smeltr writes them there.

3
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,301
You have a viewer account.

4
00:00:14,301 --> 00:00:17,510
You can open everything, and change nothing.

5
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:18,201
Open Map.

6
00:00:18,201 --> 00:00:25,135
It shows every source, pipeline and destination, and the lines records take between them.

7
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:30,002
Elasticsearch is the destination siem.

8
00:00:30,002 --> 00:00:30,910
Click it.

9
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:35,282
Show this path keeps only what feeds siem: the source firewall,

10
00:00:35,282 --> 00:00:37,670
and the pipeline firewall-parse.

11
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:39,890
Its Routes tab says it in words.

12
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:45,950
Records from firewall run through firewall-parse, and go to siem when risk is not low.

13
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,028
Click firewall-parse, and open it.

14
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:54,546
Its steps run from top to bottom.

15
00:00:54,546 --> 00:00:58,127
A parser reads the firewall line into fields.

16
00:00:58,127 --> 00:01:00,593
A drop step drops some records.

17
00:01:00,593 --> 00:01:02,821
A rename renames two fields.

18
00:01:02,821 --> 00:01:04,572
A Code step sets risk.

19
00:01:04,572 --> 00:01:08,270
And the last step removes the original line.

20
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:10,364
Check on learning.

21
00:01:10,564 --> 00:01:13,153
Your SIEM reads from one destination.

22
00:01:13,153 --> 00:01:16,762
How do you find the pipeline that feeds it?

23
00:01:21,162 --> 00:01:26,543
On Map, click the destination, and choose Show this path.

24
00:01:27,443 --> 00:01:31,494
Next, follow one record through this pipeline.
